# TODO

## -l vs -c : should -l override -c? cf l. 8419

per [issue thread](https://github.com/Genivia/ugrep/issues/457) and readme, ugrep is meant to be a drop-in replacement for GNU grep, and it currently emulates GNU grep behaviour, so perhaps the issue below is intentional.

testing the greps with e.g. 'echo foobar | <grep> -Hcl o' and 'echo foobar | <grep> -Hlc o':

- bsd-grep
  prints both, always -c first:
  (standard input):1
  (standard input)

- gnu-grep:
  -l always overrides -c:
  (standard input)

- ripgrep
  prints one or the other, with the later argument determining which is printed

- ugrep
  same as gnu-grep
